# BOHEMIA SIDE QUEST — PLAYABLE .bq (canon machine layer)
# S07 — SAY IT BACK
# Shape: slow-burn faith, NO combat (Sinnerman/Two Coins register). Faction wires:
# CHURCH, VOLUNTEERS. A dying congregant asks you to carry forgiveness to the person
# who wronged her, before she goes. The craft (W6): the game REFUSES to score whether
# forgiveness is right, earned, or wise — it just lets you carry the words, twist them,
# or not. No stat gate decides if you are "good enough" to be forgiven; there is no
# meter here at all. No proper names (W8). CLOUT rides the deed: carry it quietly ->
# #quiet; make the reconciliation a public spectacle -> #notable; deliver it as a curse
# instead -> #risky (you turned a dying woman's mercy into a loud, ugly thing, and loud
# is what the feed feeds on).

# ---- QUESTBOOK STUDY CITATIONS (QUEST STUDY LAW, 7/26/26) -------------------
# The corpus this quest is actually built from. Every id below resolves in
# records/BOHEMIA_QUESTBOOK_LAW_INDEX.json (3,672 citable findings mined out of
# the 152 studied quests in questbook/), and gates/quest_study_gate.js verifies
# the id AND the quoted law title verbatim. A citation is a claim the machine
# can check, never a name-drop.
# @STUDY Q001.N4  NODE TAMARA_MEET
#   applied: the dying woman cannot be argued with and the wronged man cannot be won; neither node has a winning line.
# @STUDY Q001.W11  NO CLEAN WIN
#   applied: carrying it true pays nothing and is seen by nobody, which is the whole point.
# @STUDY Q021.W2  "YOU CAN'T SAVE EVERYONE" AS THE THESIS
#   applied: she dies either way; the quest is only about what you set down before she does.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@QUEST   bq_say_it_back   Say It Back
@ACT     1
@FACTION NONE
@ONCE    true

@ROLE dying      REQ  faction=CHURCH   near_the_end=true
@ROLE wrongdoer  REQ  faction_any      wronged_the_dying=true

@STAGE 10
  @LOG An old woman of the church is dying and wants a message carried before she goes.
  @DO show_objective 10
@STAGE 20
  @LOG The message is forgiveness. The one she wants to forgive is the one who took her son.
  @DO show_objective 20
  @DO complete_objective 10
@STAGE 30 COMPLETE #quiet
  @LOG Carried her words, plain and true, to the man who earned none of them. He wept. Nobody else ever knew.
  @DO bond dying +20
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 31 COMPLETE #notable
  @LOG Brought them together in front of the whole block. A reconciliation with an audience. She got to see it land.
  @DO bond dying +12
  @DO faction CHURCH +6
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 32 COMPLETE #risky
  @LOG Delivered it as a curse instead of a blessing. Loud, in the open. He won't sleep, and neither, later, will I.
  @DO bond dying -20
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 33 FAIL #quiet
  @LOG Couldn't carry it. She died with the words still in her mouth. I'll wonder about that for a long time.
  @DO bond dying -5

@OBJ 10  "Sit with the dying woman"     target=dying
@OBJ 20  "Carry her message"            target=wrongdoer

@TALK bedside  speaker=dying  entry=stage>=10
  @SAY Come close, I don't have the breath to say it twice. There's one thing left and I can't walk it there myself. #tired
  @SAY There's a man. He took my son from me, years back, in the ugly way. I've carried it so long my hands ache from it.
  @SAY I want to put it down before I go. I want you to find him and tell him, in my words, that I forgive him. Say it back to me first so I know you have it.
  @OPT "I forgive you. I'll carry it."   [gate: none]  -> why     @DO learn holds_the_words
  @OPT "He doesn't deserve that."          [gate: none]  -> deserve
  @OPT "I can't promise I will."           [gate: none]  -> refuse
  @NOVERB "Tell her he is already dead"
@END

@TALK why  speaker=dying
  @SAY Deserve. You sound like the part of me that stayed angry for nine years. #dread
  @SAY It isn't for him. It never was. It's so the last thing I set down is lighter than the thing I carried. Go on. Please.
  @OPT "I'll find him."                   [gate: none]  -> deliver_true   @DO set_stage 20
  @OPT "And if I change the words on the way?"  [gate: none]  -> warn
@END

@TALK deserve  speaker=dying
  @SAY No. He doesn't. That's the whole point of the word. Cheap forgiveness is just a debt paid on time. #tired
  @SAY Mine isn't cheap. It cost me nine years. Carry it anyway, or don't, but don't pretend it's small.
  @OPT "I'll carry it."                   [gate: none]  -> deliver_true   @DO learn holds_the_words @DO set_stage 20
  @OPT "I can't."                          [gate: none]  -> refuse
@END

@TALK warn  speaker=dying
  @SAY Then it isn't my message anymore, it's yours, and you'll answer for yours the way I answered for mine. #dread
  @SAY I'm trusting a stranger with the last true thing I own. That's what dying makes you do.
  @OPT "I'll keep it true."               [gate: none]  -> deliver_true  @DO learn holds_the_words @DO set_stage 20
  @OPT "We'll see."                       [gate: none]  -> deliver_curse @DO learn holds_the_words @DO set_stage 20
@END

@TALK refuse  speaker=dying
  @SAY Then sit with me a while instead, and don't let me talk you into anything. #tired
  @OPT "I changed my mind. I'll go."      [gate: none]  -> deliver_true  @DO learn holds_the_words @DO set_stage 20
  @OPT (stay until it's over)              [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 33
@END

# THE DELIVERY. The wronged man. The game will not tell you if this was right.
@TALK deliver_true  speaker=wrongdoer  entry=stage>=20 and knows:holds_the_words
  @SAY I know that name. I've waited years for someone to come about that name, and I always thought it would be a knife. #dread
  @SAY You're telling me she forgives me. Say it again. Say it the way she said it.
  @OPT "In her words: she forgives you."   [gate: none]  -> true_end
  @OPT "Bring people. Let them see this."   [gate: none]  -> public
  @OPT "She curses you with her last breath."  [gate: none]  -> curse_end
  @NOVERB "Tell him what she really thinks of him"
@END

@TALK true_end  speaker=wrongdoer
  @SAY (he doesn't speak for a long time. when he does it isn't words, it's the sound a man makes when a weight he's carried alone is suddenly shared.)
  @SAY Nobody will ever know you did this. It doesn't pay. Thank you for doing it anyway.
  @OPT "She wanted it lighter. Now it is."  [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 30
  @OPT (leave him to it)                    [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 30
@END

@TALK public  speaker=wrongdoer
  @SAY You brought a crowd. For this. #dread
  @SAY I'll say the right things because they're watching, and she'll see it land while she can still see. But you and I both know what you traded to make it a show.
  @OPT "Let her see it land."               [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 31
@END

@TALK deliver_curse  speaker=wrongdoer  entry=stage>=20 and knows:holds_the_words
  @SAY I know that name. I've waited years. Tell me what she wants me to hear. #dread
  @OPT "She curses you with her last breath."  [gate: none]  -> curse_end
  @OPT "No. The truth. She forgives you."      [gate: none]  -> true_end
@END

@TALK curse_end  speaker=wrongdoer
  @SAY (something in him that was trying to open slams shut, and hardens, and will stay hard now.)
  @SAY Then I'll carry it the rest of my life exactly as heavy as it was. You did that. Not her. You.
  @OPT "So be it."                          [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 32
  @OPT (walk away from what you made)        [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 32
@END
